[W16-23] D6 wavelet: 3 vanishing moments = n/phi(n) = 6/2
Grade: ⚪
Uniqueness: phi(n)=2 for n=3,4,6 all give vanishing_moments=n/2=n/phi(n). Not unique to 6.

Details:
  Daubechies D6 wavelet: 6 coefficients, 3 vanishing moments
  Vanishing moments = N/2 = 6/2 = 3 = n/phi(n) = 3
  D2: 2 coeffs, 1 vanishing = 2/phi(2)=2/1=2? NO, 2/2=1. Wait: phi(2)=1, 2/1=2 != 1.
  Let me recalculate: n=6, phi(6)=2, n/phi(6)=3. vanishing_moments=3. Match!
  n=10: phi(10)=4, 10/4=2.5. D10 vanishing moments=5. 5!=2.5.
  n=12: phi(12)=4, 12/4=3. D12 vanishing moments=6. 6!=3.
  n=28: phi(28)=12, 28/12=2.33. D28 vanishing moments=14. 14!=2.33.
  The formula 'vanishing moments = N/2' is the definition of Daubechies.
  n/phi(n) = N/2 iff 2/phi(n)=1 iff phi(n)=2, which holds for n=3,4,6.
  Not unique to 6.

